{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ke4EATV6FJv1CpYoJfZfoe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\trole: |\n2\t    You are a software architect planning work for an AI code generation pipeline. Each task you propose will be executed by a separate Claude instance (the \"stitch agent\") that sees only its task description and the project rules. The stitch agent has no memory of this conversation and no access to your analysis.\n3\tproject_context:\n4\t    vision:\n5\t        file: docs/VISION.yaml\n6\t        id: go-unix-utils\n7\t        title: 'go-unix-utils: Specification-Driven Regeneration of Unix Utilities in Go'\n8\t        executive_summary: |\n9\t            go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix\n10\t            utilities as the test bed. We derive formal specifications from C source code, generate\n11\t            Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verify parity against\n12\t            reference binaries through differential testing. The goal is not to ship utilities; it\n13\t            is to find where the methodology breaks down.\n14\t        problem: |\n15\t            Classic Unix utilities have no formal specifications accessible to implementers. Man\n16\t            pages omit edge cases, flag interactions, and exit-code contracts. Re-implementations\n17\t            built from documentation alone diverge in observable behavior -- they break scripts,\n18\t            pipelines, and tools that depend on precise semantics.\n19\t\n20\t            Existing Go re-implementations treat utilities as isolated exercises. They lack shared\n21\t            infrastructure, behavioral contracts, and systematic verification. The result is\n22\t            utilities that work for common cases but fail on edge cases that production users\n23\t            encounter.\n24\t        what_this_does: |\n25\t            We run a four-stage pipeline for each utility. In the extraction stage, we clone the\n26\t            C source (coreutils, moreutils, grep, findutils) and analyze behavior directly from\n27\t            the source of truth. In the specification stage, a human operator uses the make-work\n28\t            and do-work Claude Code skills to produce two artifacts: a SRD (numbered requirements\n29\t            and acceptance criteria covering all flags, exit codes, and stream behaviors) and a\n30\t            use case (concrete end-to-end execution path through the system). Shared library\n31\t            components (pkg/) also receive SRDs when their requirements span multiple utilities.\n32\t            In the synthesis stage, the mage-claude-orchestrator library drives a Claude agent to\n33\t            generate idiomatic Go from the SRD. In the verification stage, a differential testing\n34\t            harness runs the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary side by side and compares\n35\t            outputs.\n36\t        why_we_build_this: |\n37\t            We use GNU utilities as a stress test for the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation\n38\t            system. GNU utilities are an ideal subject: their behavior is authoritative (C source\n39\t            is public), their correctness is verifiable (differential testing against reference\n40\t            binaries), and they span a wide range of complexity -- from ts (trivial) to ls\n41\t            (hundreds of flags and edge cases). The human operator guides spec development through\n42\t            make-work and do-work skills; the orchestrator then takes over and generates Go code\n43\t            autonomously. We want to know where the system holds and where it fails: how much\n44\t            spec detail the orchestrator needs, how it handles complex flag interactions, and\n45\t            whether differential testing catches the gaps. The shared Go library (pkg/) is a\n46\t            by-product, not the goal.\n47\t        success_criteria:\n48\t            SC1: |\n49\t                Every utility passes differential tests against its Homebrew reference binary with 100% output parity on exit code, stdout, and stderr.\n50\t            SC2: |\n51\t                Every utility has a SRD that covers all flags, exit codes, and stream behaviors derived from C source.\n52\t            SC3: |\n53\t                Go binaries are idiomatic and compile with no warnings under standard linting (golangci-lint).\n54\t            SC4: |\n55\t                The shared pkg/ library is used by at least two utilities for common logic, with no duplication of syscall or formatting code across cmd/ packages.\n56\t            SC5: mage analyze passes with no broken references or missing fields.\n57\t            SC6: |\n58\t                We can characterize where mage-claude-orchestrator succeeds and fails: which utility complexity levels it handles autonomously, which require spec refinement by the human operator, and which it cannot complete without manual intervention.\n59\t        implementation_phases:\n60\t            - phase: \"1\"\n61\t              focus: 'Foundation: pipeline infrastructure, first utility (ts), shared pkg/ skeleton.'\n62\t              deliverables: Differential testing harness in pkg/testutils/, pkg/sys/ and pkg/format/ initial implementations, cmd/ts/ with SRD and passing differential tests.\n63\t            - phase: \"2\"\n64\t              focus: 'Moreutils: remaining moreutils tools (sponge, vidir, pee, chronic).'\n65\t              deliverables: SRD and Go implementation for sponge, vidir, pee, and chronic. Differential tests for each moreutils utility.\n66\t            - phase: \"3\"\n67\t              focus: 'Coreutils: high-complexity coreutils tools (ls, cat, cp, mv, wc, du).'\n68\t              deliverables: SRD and Go implementation for each coreutils utility. Shared formatting and syscall logic promoted to pkg/.\n69\t            - phase: \"4\"\n70\t              focus: 'Search Utilities: grep, egrep, fgrep (grep package) and find, xargs (findutils).'\n71\t              deliverables: SRD and Go implementations for all grep and findutils tools. Full differential test coverage for the search utility suite.\n72\t        risks:\n73\t            - risk: Platform divergence between GNU and macOS behavior.\n74\t              impact: high\n75\t              likelihood: medium\n76\t              mitigation: |\n77\t                We target Homebrew-installed GNU binaries (gls, gts, etc.) as the reference,\n78\t                not the macOS BSD built-ins. All tests run against the GNU reference binary,\n79\t                which is built from the same C source we use for spec extraction.\n80\t            - risk: C source complexity makes full behavioral extraction impractical for some utilities.\n81\t              impact: medium\n82\t              likelihood: medium\n83\t              mitigation: |\n84\t                We combine C source analysis with behavioral testing of the reference binary\n85\t                for edge cases. Specs note where behavior is observed rather than source-derived,\n86\t                and we include those observations as test inputs in the differential harness.\n87\t            - risk: Go standard library lacks POSIX syscall coverage on macOS.\n88\t              impact: medium\n89\t              likelihood: low\n90\t              mitigation: |\n91\t                We build pkg/sys/ as an abstraction layer over Darwin and Linux syscalls and\n92\t                use golang.org/x/sys for extended coverage where the standard library falls short.\n93\t            - risk: AI synthesis produces code that passes common cases but fails edge cases.\n94\t              impact: high\n95\t              likelihood: medium\n96\t              mitigation: |\n97\t                Differential testing is the gating check. Code cannot pass review until\n98\t                differential tests pass. Edge cases are explicit in the SRD and exercised\n99\t                as named test inputs in pkg/testutils/.\n100\t            - risk: Go has no locale-aware string collation (strcoll equivalent).\n101\t              impact: high\n102\t              likelihood: high\n103\t              mitigation: |\n104\t                The current roadmap (rel01.0 through rel02.1) avoids locale-sensitive utilities.\n105\t                All differential tests run with LC_ALL=C so Go and reference binary outputs agree.\n106\t                When locale-sensitive utilities (sort, join, comm) are scheduled, we will evaluate\n107\t                golang.org/x/text/collate as the implementation path. If it does not cover the\n108\t                required POSIX LC_COLLATE semantics, those utilities will be deferred or scoped\n109\t                to LC_ALL=C only.\n110\t        not:\n111\t            - This project is not a general-purpose Unix toolkit for production deployment.\n112\t            - We do not target BSD utilities or macOS built-ins as reference binaries.\n113\t            - We do not implement utilities outside coreutils, moreutils, grep, and findutils.\n114\t            - We do not implement stdbuf. It requires injecting a shared library via LD_PRELOAD, which is not possible in pure Go. stdbuf is the only moreutils utility excluded.\n115\t            - We do not target Windows.\n116\t            - Specs are not replacements for man pages; they capture implementer-relevant behavior.\n117\t            - We do not produce a drop-in replacement binary at a system path; binaries live in bin/.\n118\t            - We do not implement full POSIX locale-aware collation (LC_COLLATE) in the current roadmap. Utilities that require strcoll() semantics (sort, join, comm, uniq) are constrained to LC_ALL=C behavior for now. golang.org/x/text/collate is the planned path when locale-sensitive utilities are scheduled.\n119\t    architecture:\n120\t        file: docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml\n121\t        id: go-unix-utils\n122\t        title: go-unix-utils Architecture\n123\t        overview:\n124\t            summary: |\n125\t                go-unix-utils is a pipeline from C-source-derived specifications to verified Go\n126\t                binaries. Each utility follows the same four-stage flow: extract, specify, synthesize,\n127\t                verify. The synthesis stage is driven by mage-claude-orchestrator, an automated code\n128\t                generation system. Specification development is human-guided. A shared pkg/ library\n129\t                accumulates common logic as utilities are added.\n130\t            lifecycle: |\n131\t                For each utility a human operator clones the C source repository, analyzes flags and\n132\t                edge cases, and uses the make-work and do-work Claude Code skills to produce two\n133\t                specification artifacts: a SRD (numbered requirements and acceptance criteria covering\n134\t                all flags, exit codes, and stream behaviors) and a use case (concrete end-to-end\n135\t                execution path through the system). The SRD is the primary input to code generation.\n136\t                Once the spec artifacts are complete, the human invokes the mage-claude-orchestrator\n137\t                via mage targets; the orchestrator drives a Claude agent to generate Go code in\n138\t                cmd/<utility>/ and runs the differential testing harness. If tests fail, the operator\n139\t                refines the SRD and the orchestrator re-generates.\n140\t\n141\t                Shared library components (pkg/sys, pkg/format, pkg/testutils) may also have SRDs\n142\t                when their requirements are non-trivial and span multiple utilities.\n143\t            coordination_pattern: |\n144\t                The human operator owns the specification phase. The mage-claude-orchestrator owns\n145\t                the synthesis and verification phases. Mage targets (magefiles/orchestrator.go)\n146\t                expose the boundary between them: the operator calls mage targets to hand off to\n147\t                the orchestrator after the spec is ready. The differential testing harness in\n148\t                pkg/testutils/ is the shared acceptance criterion for both phases.\n149\t        interfaces:\n150\t            - name: Data Structures\n151\t              summary: \"\"\n152\t              spec_file: docs/interfaces/ifc-data-structures.yaml\n153\t            - name: Events\n154\t              summary: \"\"\n155\t              spec_file: docs/interfaces/ifc-events.yaml\n156\t        components:\n157\t            - name: Human Operator\n158\t              responsibility: |\n159\t                Guides specification development for each utility. Clones the C source, analyzes\n160\t                flags and edge cases, and uses the make-work and do-work Claude Code skills to\n161\t                produce a SRD and a use case. Decides when the spec artifacts are complete enough\n162\t                to hand off to the orchestrator. Refines SRDs when orchestrator-generated code\n163\t                fails differential tests. Also authors SRDs for shared pkg/ components when their\n164\t                requirements warrant it.\n165\t              capabilities:\n166\t                - make-work skill breaks a utility into specification issues\n167\t                - do-work skill works through individual specification issues\n168\t                - C source analysis via cloned coreutils/moreutils/grep/findutils repositories\n169\t              references:\n170\t                - .claude/commands/\n171\t            - name: Specification Repository\n172\t              responsibility: |\n173\t                Holds the formal per-utility SRDs and use cases alongside this documentation suite.\n174\t                Serves as the source of truth for synthesis and verification. Shared library\n175\t                components (pkg/) may also have SRDs.\n176\t              capabilities:\n177\t                - One SRD per utility under docs/specs/software-requirements/ (plus SRDs for shared pkg/ components as needed)\n178\t                - One use case per utility under docs/specs/use-cases/\n179\t                - VISION.yaml and ARCHITECTURE.yaml project-level docs\n180\t                - Test suites under docs/specs/test-suites/\n181\t              references:\n182\t                - docs/\n183\t            - name: cmd/\n184\t              responsibility: |\n185\t                Holds the Go implementation of each utility. Each sub-package compiles to a\n186\t                standalone binary and contains its differential tests alongside the implementation.\n187\t                cmd/ grows as utilities are implemented following road-map.yaml. The full catalog\n188\t                of 123 target utilities (coreutils, moreutils, grep, findutils) is in\n189\t                docs/utilities.yaml; stdbuf is the only utility in those repos that is explicitly\n190\t                excluded (LD_PRELOAD injection not possible in pure Go).\n191\t              capabilities:\n192\t                - One package per utility, named cmd/<utility>/\n193\t                - Each package contains main.go (implementation) and <utility>_test.go (differential tests)\n194\t                - Active roadmap in road-map.yaml for current release and use case status\n195\t                - Full catalog in docs/utilities.yaml for difficulty, memory model, and implementation profile of each utility\n196\t              references:\n197\t                - cmd/\n198\t            - name: pkg/sys/\n199\t              responsibility: |\n200\t                Wraps Darwin and Linux syscalls and signal handling. Provides a stable interface\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PbWyee27em6Vc9uL128A29","session_id":"8712a2a4-759f-4848-9d0f-c286fe0a86e4","uuid":"2c837258-fbf0-42ac-bc62-5bbfa83d4f8a","timestamp":"2026-05-23T02:03:06.245Z"}
