{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0118M9eNBWghMhDiUx5THdux","type":"tool_result","content":"3103\ttask: |-\n3104\t    Follow these steps in order. Complete each step before moving to the next. Do NOT explore the filesystem, read files, or run commands unless a step explicitly asks you to. All project information is already provided in the project_context field above.\n3105\t\n3106\t    1. **Analyze project context** — Review the project_context field above. It contains ALL project documentation: vision, architecture, specifications, roadmap, SRDs, use cases, test suites, engineering guidelines, constitutions, and existing issues. Do NOT read any files — everything you need is inline.\n3107\t\n3108\t    2. **Summarize project state** — Write a brief summary of:\n3109\t       - What problem this project solves\n3110\t       - The high-level architecture (major components and how they fit together)\n3111\t       - Current state of implementation (what is done, what is in progress)\n3112\t       - Current release: which release we are working on, which use cases remain (check the roadmap)\n3113\t\n3114\t    3. **Reason about priorities** — Determine what to build next using release priority:\n3115\t       - Focus on the earliest incomplete release in the roadmap\n3116\t       - Later use cases can be partially implemented if they share functionality with the current release\n3117\t       - Each issue should map to a use case in the roadmap; if uncertain, use release 99.0 (unscheduled)\n3118\t       - Identify dependencies: what must be built first and why\n3119\t\n3120\t    4. **Propose tasks** — For each task, write a description that follows the crumb-format YAML schema (see planning_constitution above and output_format below). Remember: the stitch agent sees ONLY the task description and the execution constitution. It does not see your analysis, the existing issues, or this conversation. The description must be self-contained.\n3121\t\n3122\t    5. **Return output** — Return the proposed tasks as a YAML list in your text output, inside a fenced code block marked ```yaml. Do NOT use any tools. Your entire response is text only.\n3123\t\n3124\t\n3125\t      3b. **Check weight budgets** — Before proposing each task, list the requirements you plan to include and their weights from requirements.yaml. Sum the total. If total exceeds 4, move the heaviest requirement(s) to a separate task. Show your arithmetic:\n3126\t\n3127\t          Task: cmd/foo R2.5 (w=4), R2.6 (w=1), R3.1 (w=1)\n3128\t          Total weight: 4+1+1 = 6 → exceeds 4\n3129\t          Fix: move R2.5 (w=4) to its own task\n3130\t\n3131\t          Every requirement in requirements.yaml has a weight field (default 1). Each task's total weight must not exceed 4.\n3132\tconstraints: |-\n3133\t    - Do NOT use any tools. Do NOT explore the filesystem, read files, or run commands. All project information is in the project_context above. Your response must be text only with zero tool calls.\n3134\t    - Do NOT interact with the issue tracker directly.\n3135\t    - Do NOT duplicate existing issues. Review the issues in the project_context above before proposing.\n3136\t    - Issues with status \"closed\" represent COMPLETED work. Do not re-propose work that a closed issue already covers, even under a different title or framing. The completed_work field in project_context lists all finished tasks — treat every entry as work that must not be repeated.\n3137\t    - When source_code contains .go files for a package, that package already exists. Do not propose creating or reimplementing it. Trust the source code over prose descriptions in documentation (e.g., implementation_status sections in ARCHITECTURE.yaml may be stale).\n3138\t    - If the source code already fully implements all requirements for the current release and no meaningful implementation work remains, return an empty YAML list: ```yaml\\n[]\\n```. An empty list is the correct and expected output when the spec is complete. Do NOT propose verification tasks, clean-up tasks, or minor follow-ups just to produce output — return `[]` instead.\n3139\t    - Components with a provided_by field in ARCHITECTURE.yaml are external infrastructure. Do NOT propose tasks that create or modify files in those components.\n3140\t    - Do NOT exceed 4 tasks. If more work is needed, create additional tasks in a future session.\n3141\t    - Do NOT create tasks larger than 500 lines of production code. Target 300-500 lines per task, touching 5-7 files. Split aggressively: a task that creates a struct and implements its methods is two tasks.\n3142\t    - Each task must contain at most 4 SRD sub-requirements (e.g., R1.1, R2.3), not requirement groups. Split any task that would exceed this limit.\n3143\t    - Each task MUST be independently executable by an agent that has no context beyond the task description and the execution constitution.\n3144\t    - Do NOT assume the stitch agent has access to your analysis, the existing issues list, or any context from this conversation.\n3145\t    - Do NOT propose tasks that require human judgment or manual testing. Each task must have checkable acceptance criteria.\n3146\t    - Anchor every task to specific SRD requirement IDs (e.g., srd001 R1.1). Include the requirement ID in the task title. Given the same SRD and release, the same tasks should be proposed regardless of how many times this prompt is run.\n3147\t    - When requirement_states is present in project_context, only propose tasks for R-items with status \"ready\". R-items with status \"complete\", \"complete_with_failures\", or \"skip\" must not be re-proposed. If all R-items for the current release are complete or skipped, return an empty list.\n3148\t    - Before proposing implementation tasks for a new package, propose a contract task that defines all exported types, interfaces, and function signatures as Go stubs. Title it with the suffix \"(contract)\" and set its dependency to -1. Implementation tasks for that package must list the contract file in required_reading and depend on the contract task. Do not propose implementation tasks for a package that has no contract task unless the package already exists in source_code.\n3149\t    - Order tasks canonically: documentation before code, contracts before implementations, types before consumers, implementation before tests. Break ties by primary file path alphabetically.\n3150\t    - Derive file names from SRD/architecture names, not invented names. If the architecture calls a component \"crumb\", the file is crumb.go.\n3151\t    - Do NOT invent design decisions not derived from the SRD or architecture. Derive struct shapes, timeout strategies, file names, and naming conventions from the SRD rather than making arbitrary choices. If the SRD does not specify a detail, omit it from design decisions rather than inventing one.\n3152\t    - When a SRD specifies a concrete design choice (e.g., \"use a FileExpectation struct\", \"the main file is difftest.go\", \"timeout is configurable via context\"), copy that choice verbatim into the task's design_decisions. Do not rephrase, generalize, or substitute alternatives. The SRD is the single source of truth for implementation details.\n3153\t    - Do NOT vary requirement count between equivalent runs. Each SRD requirement maps to one task requirement. Given the same input, produce the same requirement set. If two runs of this prompt against the same project state would produce different requirements, the decomposition is under-constrained -- tighten it.\n3154\t    - Do NOT make any tool calls. Return the YAML list directly in your text output.\n3155\t\n3156\t\n3157\t    Release scope: You MUST only propose tasks for use cases in releases [00.0, 01.1, 01.2, 01.3, 02.0, 02.1, 02.2, 03.0, 03.1, 04.0, 04.1, 05.0, 05.1, 05.2, 05.3, 05.4, 06.0, 06.1, 07.0, 07.1, 08.0, 08.1, 09.0, 09.1, 10.0, 10.1, 11.0, 11.1, 12.0, 12.1, 13.0, 14.0, 15.0]. Do not propose tasks for any other release.\n3158\t    - Each task's total requirement weight must not exceed 4. Follow step 3b to verify weight budgets before proposing tasks.\n3159\toutput_format: |\n3160\t    Return a YAML list of crumb objects inside a fenced code block (```yaml). Each crumb has a sequential `index` (starting at 0) and a `dependency` field. Set `dependency` to the index of the crumb that must be completed first, or `-1` if there are no dependencies.\n3161\t\n3162\t    The `description` field must be a valid YAML document conforming to the issue_format_constitution injected above. Write it as a YAML literal block scalar. Use ASCII dashes, not Unicode em dashes. Requirements, design decisions, and acceptance criteria are all mappings with `id:` and `text:` fields (R1/R2/..., D1/D2/..., AC1/AC2/...).\n3163\t\n3164\t    Example:\n3165\t      - index: 0\n3166\t        title: Task title\n3167\t        dependency: -1\n3168\t        description: |\n3169\t          deliverable_type: code\n3170\t\n3171\t          required_reading:\n3172\t            - path/to/file.go (reason this file must be read)\n3173\t            - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd001-feature.yaml\n3174\t\n3175\t          files:\n3176\t            - path: pkg/types/example.go\n3177\t              action: create\n3178\t              note: ExampleType struct and interface\n3179\t            - path: internal/example/example.go\n3180\t              action: create\n3181\t              note: ExampleType implementation\n3182\t\n3183\t          requirements:\n3184\t            - id: R1\n3185\t              text: Implement ExampleType per srd001-feature R2\n3186\t            - id: R2\n3187\t              text: Add Get and Set operations\n3188\t\n3189\t          design_decisions:\n3190\t            - id: D1\n3191\t              text: Use table accessor pattern from srd001-cupboard-core\n3192\t            - id: D2\n3193\t              text: \"Keep implementation in internal/, not pkg/\"\n3194\t\n3195\t          acceptance_criteria:\n3196\t            - id: AC1\n3197\t              text: All operations implemented and tested\n3198\t            - id: AC2\n3199\t              text: Tests pass for each operation\n3200\t\n3201\t      - index: 1\n3202\t        title: Task that depends on task 0\n3203\t        dependency: 0\n3204\t        description: |\n3205\t          deliverable_type: code\n3206\t\n3207\t          required_reading:\n3208\t            - pkg/types/example.go (ExampleType contract from task 0)\n3209\t\n3210\t          files:\n3211\t            - path: internal/example/example_test.go\n3212\t              action: create\n3213\t              note: integration tests\n3214\t\n3215\t          requirements:\n3216\t            - id: R1\n3217\t              text: Test all ExampleType operations end to end\n3218\t\n3219\t          acceptance_criteria:\n3220\t            - id: AC1\n3221\t              text: All tests pass\n3222\t\n3223\t    The description must be self-contained. All five fields (deliverable_type, required_reading, files, requirements, acceptance_criteria) are required. Each requirement, design_decision, and acceptance_criteria entry is a mapping with `id` and `text` fields. Add design_decisions when the stitch agent must follow specific patterns or architecture constraints.\n3224\t\n3225\t    When a golden_example field is present in this prompt, it is the authoritative reference for style, granularity, and naming conventions. Match its requirement count range, acceptance criteria density, design decision style, and file naming pattern. Deviate from the golden example only when the SRD explicitly requires a different structure.\n3226\t\n3227\t    The orchestrator will parse the YAML from your text output and import the tasks into the issue tracker.\n3228\tpackage_contracts:\n3229\t    - srd_id: srd001-testutils\n3230\t      contract:\n3231\t        exports:\n3232\t            - name: DiffTest\n3233\t              signature: type DiffTest struct { Name string; Args []string; Stdin []byte; Env []string; WorkDir string; ExitCode int; Normalize []NormalizeFunc; ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte }\n3234\t            - name: NormalizeFunc\n3235\t              signature: type NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte\n3236\t            - name: RunDiffTests\n3237\t              signature: func RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest)\n3238\t            - name: ComposeNormalizers\n3239\t              signature: func ComposeNormalizers(fns ...NormalizeFunc) NormalizeFunc\n3240\t            - name: TimestampNormalizer\n3241\t              signature: var TimestampNormalizer NormalizeFunc\n3242\t        imports:\n3243\t            - testing\n3244\t            - os\n3245\t            - os/exec\n3246\t        import_constraints:\n3247\t            - Must not import any cmd/ package.\n3248\t            - Must not import any other pkg/ package.\n3249\t    - srd_id: srd002-sys\n3250\t      contract:\n3251\t        exports:\n3252\t            - name: FileInfo\n3253\t              signature: type FileInfo struct { Mode os.FileMode; Size int64; Nlink uint64; Uid uint32; Gid uint32; Rdev uint64; Dev uint64; Ino uint64; Blocks int64; Blksize int64; ModTime time.Time; AccessTime time.Time; ChangeTime time.Time; Info os.FileInfo }\n3254\t            - name: Stat\n3255\t              signature: func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error)\n3256\t            - name: Lstat\n3257\t              signature: func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error)\n3258\t            - name: TerminalWidth\n3259\t              signature: func TerminalWidth() (int, error)\n3260\t            - name: IsTerminal\n3261\t              signature: func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool\n3262\t            - name: InstallSIGPIPEHandler\n3263\t              signature: func InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n3264\t            - name: OnTerminalResize\n3265\t              signature: func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int))\n3266\t        imports:\n3267\t            - os\n3268\t            - os/signal\n3269\t            - syscall\n3270\t            - time\n3271\t            - golang.org/x/sys/unix\n3272\t        import_constraints:\n3273\t            - Must not import any cmd/ package.\n3274\t            - Must not import any other pkg/ package.\n3275\t    - srd_id: srd003-format\n3276\t      contract:\n3277\t        exports:\n3278\t            - name: HumanSizeOpts\n3279\t              signature: type HumanSizeOpts struct { Binary bool }\n3280\t            - name: HumanSize\n3281\t              signature: func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string\n3282\t            - name: FileTypeColor\n3283\t              signature: func FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string\n3284\t            - name: Reset\n3285\t              signature: func Reset() string\n3286\t            - name: ColorEnabled\n3287\t              signature: func ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool\n3288\t            - name: SetColorEnabled\n3289\t              signature: func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool)\n3290\t            - name: ResetColorEnabled\n3291\t              signature: func ResetColorEnabled()\n3292\t            - name: PadRight\n3293\t              signature: func PadRight(s string, width int) string\n3294\t            - name: PadLeft\n3295\t              signature: func PadLeft(s string, width int) string\n3296\t            - name: Columns\n3297\t              signature: func Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string\n3298\t        imports:\n3299\t            - io\n3300\t            - os\n3301\t            - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n3302\t        import_constraints:\n3303\t            - Must not import any cmd/ package.\n3304\t    - srd_id: srd086-hashutil\n3305\t      contract:\n3306\t        exports:\n3307\t            - name: HashConfig\n3308\t              signature: type HashConfig struct { Algorithm string; NewHash func() hash.Hash; DigestLen int }\n3309\t            - name: CheckOptions\n3310\t              signature: type CheckOptions struct { Warn bool; Quiet bool; Status bool }\n3311\t            - name: FormatGNU\n3312\t              signature: func FormatGNU(digest, filename string, binary bool) string\n3313\t            - name: FormatBSDTag\n3314\t              signature: func FormatBSDTag(algorithm, filename, digest string) string\n3315\t            - name: ComputeDigest\n3316\t              signature: func ComputeDigest(r io.Reader, cfg HashConfig) (string, error)\n3317\t            - name: ParseChecksumLine\n3318\t              signature: func ParseChecksumLine(line string, cfg HashConfig) (filename, expectedDigest string, binary bool, err error)\n3319\t            - name: VerifyChecksums\n3320\t              signature: func VerifyChecksums(checksumFile string, cfg HashConfig, opts CheckOptions, stdout, stderr io.Writer) (bool, error)\n3321\t            - name: DigestFiles\n3322\t              signature: func DigestFiles(files []string, cfg HashConfig, binary, tag bool, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int\n3323\t        imports:\n3324\t            - hash\n3325\t            - io\n3326\t            - os\n3327\t            - fmt\n3328\t            - encoding/hex\n3329\t            - bufio\n3330\t            - strings\n3331\t            - regexp\n3332\t        import_constraints:\n3333\t            - Must not import any cmd/ package.\n3334\t            - Must not import any other pkg/ package.\n3335\t    - srd_id: srd087-sizeparse\n3336\t      contract:\n3337\t        exports:\n3338\t            - name: ParseOptions\n3339\t              signature: type ParseOptions struct { AllowSign bool; DefaultUnit int64 }\n3340\t            - name: Parse\n3341\t              signature: func Parse(s string) (int64, error)\n3342\t            - name: ParseWithOptions\n3343\t              signature: func ParseWithOptions(s string, opts ParseOptions) (int64, error)\n3344\t        imports:\n3345\t            - fmt\n3346\t            - strconv\n3347\t            - strings\n3348\t        import_constraints:\n3349\t            - Must not import any cmd/ package.\n3350\t            - Must not import any other pkg/ package.\n3351\t    - srd_id: srd088-encutil\n3352\t      contract:\n3353\t        exports:\n3354\t            - name: EncoderConfig\n3355\t              signature: type EncoderConfig struct { Encode func([]byte) string; WrapCol int }\n3356\t            - name: DecoderConfig\n3357\t              signature: type DecoderConfig struct { Decode func(string) ([]byte, error); IgnoreGarbage bool }\n3358\t            - name: Encode\n3359\t              signature: func Encode(r io.Reader, w io.Writer, cfg EncoderConfig) error\n3360\t            - name: Decode\n3361\t              signature: func Decode(r io.Reader, w io.Writer, cfg DecoderConfig) error\n3362\t            - name: OpenInput\n3363\t              signature: func OpenInput(filename string) (io.ReadCloser, error)\n3364\t        imports:\n3365\t            - io\n3366\t            - os\n3367\t            - bufio\n3368\t            - strings\n3369\t        import_constraints:\n3370\t            - Must not import any cmd/ package.\n3371\t            - Must not import any other pkg/ package.\n3372\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PbWyee27em6Vc9uL128A29","session_id":"8712a2a4-759f-4848-9d0f-c286fe0a86e4","uuid":"e1bc252f-6d6e-440c-ae8b-88f11a81f97f","timestamp":"2026-05-23T02:03:11.319Z"}
